{
  "dom_id": "step-q95136",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "The systolic blood pressure of a group of people is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and a standard deviation of $12\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$ Additionally, $90%$ of the people have a systolic blood pressure between $0.82\\mu \\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and $1.18\\mu \\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$ Find the mean systolic blood pressure of this group, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the systolic blood pressure of a randomly selected patient. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{12}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.82\\mu <X<1.18\\mu )=0.9.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.82\\mu <X<1.18\\mu ) & =0.9 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.18\\mu ) & =0.9 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.18\\mu ) & =0.45 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.18\\mu ) & =0.5+0.45 \\\\ P(X<1.18\\mu ) & =0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.18\\mu ) & =0.95 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.18\\mu }{12}) & =0.95 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.18\\mu }{12}) & =0.95 \\\\ \\frac{0.18\\mu }{12} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.95)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.95)≈1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.18\\mu }{12} & =1.6449 \\\\ 0.18\\mu  & =19.7388 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{19.7388}{0.18} \\\\  & ≈109.66.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
